[{"text":"CartesianPair[a, b] is a special pairing used in the internal representation. a and b may have heads CartesianIndex or CartesianMomentum. If both a and b have head CartesianIndex, the Kronecker delta is understood. If a and b have head CartesianMomentum, a Cartesian scalar product is meant. If one of a and b has head CartesianIndex and the other CartesianMomentum, a Cartesian vector p^i is understood.","anchor":"cartesianpair"},{"text":"Overview, Pair, TemporalPair.","anchor":"see-also","section":"See also"},{"text":"This represents a three-dimensional Kronecker delta","anchor":"examples","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i], CartesianIndex[j]]","anchor":"cb1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\delta }^{ij}\\]","anchor":"cb1-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"This is a \\(D-1\\)-dimensional Kronecker delta","anchor":"cb1-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i, D - 1], CartesianIndex[j, D - 1]]","anchor":"cb2","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\delta ^{ij}\\]","anchor":"cb2-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"If the Cartesian indices live in different dimensions, this gets resolved according to the t’Hoft-Veltman-Breitenlohner-Maison prescription","anchor":"cb2-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i, D - 1], CartesianIndex[j]]","anchor":"cb3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\delta }^{ij}\\]","anchor":"cb3-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i, D - 1], CartesianIndex[j, D - 4]]","anchor":"cb4","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\hat{\\delta }^{ij}\\]","anchor":"cb4-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i], CartesianIndex[j, D - 4]]","anchor":"cb5","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[0\\]","anchor":"cb5-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"A \\(3\\)-dimensional Cartesian vector","anchor":"cb5-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i], CartesianMomentum[p]]","anchor":"cb6","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^i\\]","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"A \\(D-1\\)-dimensional Cartesian vector","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianIndex[i, D - 1], CartesianMomentum[p, D - 1]]","anchor":"cb7","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[p^i\\]","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\(3\\)-dimensional scalar products of Cartesian vectors","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianMomentum[q], CartesianMomentum[p]]","anchor":"cb8","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}\\cdot \\overline{q}\\]","anchor":"cb8-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianMomentum[p], CartesianMomentum[p]]","anchor":"cb9","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^2\\]","anchor":"cb9-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianMomentum[p - q], CartesianMomentum[p]]","anchor":"cb10","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}\\cdot (\\overline{p}-\\overline{q})\\]","anchor":"cb10-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianMomentum[p], CartesianMomentum[p]]^2","anchor":"cb11","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^4\\]","anchor":"cb11-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"CartesianPair[CartesianMomentum[p], CartesianMomentum[p]]^3","anchor":"cb12","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^6\\]","anchor":"cb12-1","section":"Examples"}]